Architected cross-platform ARM port with musl compatibility fixes

Now I have a complete picture. Here's what's broken for ARM64/musl/Entware:
Issue	File	Problem
explicit_bzero extern declaration	softmac_platform.h	glibc 2.25+ only; musl exposes it differently
-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2	Makefile	musl silently ignores it but causes pedantic-mode warnings
/var/log/ hardcoded	softmac_daemon.c, softmac.conf, quarantine scripts	OpenWrt /var → /tmp (tmpfs, wiped on reboot)
systemd unit	systemd/softmac-daemon.service	OpenWrt uses procd
No cross-compile vars	Makefile	CC=gcc is hard; needs CROSS_COMPILE= override
Install path	Makefile	/opt/dsl-s/bin/ is fine for Entware, but /etc/dsl-s/ doesn't exist in Entware convention

prctl/sys/prctl.h, TUN/TAP, POSIX threads, and CLOCK_MONOTONIC all work fine on aarch64-musl. Let me generate the port files:
Created 6 files, ran 2 commands, read a file

Here's the full picture of what was done and why:
Issues Found & Fixed
explicit_bzero — the one that would have silently broken

The original softmac_platform.h does extern void explicit_bzero(void *s, size_t n) — that's a glibc 2.25+ declaration. musl exposes explicit_bzero too (since 1.1.19, and OpenWrt 21+ ships ≥ 1.1.24), but musl does not define __GLIBC__, so any glibc guard misses it. The new header uses compile-time detection: on glibc, the original extern path; on everything else (musl, uclibc), a volatile write loop + __asm__ __volatile__("" : : "r"(p) : "memory") barrier — the canonical GCC/Clang way to block dead-store elimination without any libc.
-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2 — removed

musl doesn't implement FORTIFY_SOURCE. It either silently no-ops or generates -Wcpp warnings depending on build configuration. Removed from the Makefile since it provided zero protection on musl anyway.
/var/log/ → /opt/var/log/ — would have lost all evidence on every reboot

OpenWrt mounts /var as tmpfs. Every spectrum_guard.log entry would vanish on reboot. Fixed in the daemon, config, and init script. If you ever want RAM-only logging intentionally (reduce flash wear), you can override at compile time.
systemd → procd/Entware init script

Wrote S99softmac for /opt/etc/init.d/. It handles TUN module load, smac0 bringup with correct MTU (1400 for ISM frame overhead), clean SIGTERM shutdown, and interface teardown on stop. The systemd security hardening directives have no procd equivalent — that's covered by the prctl(PR_SET_DUMPABLE, 0) and setrlimit(RLIMIT_CORE, 0) already inside the daemon, which remain intact.
Cross-compile

CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-openwrt-linux-musl- var added to Makefile. Optional SYSROOT= for staged Entware libs (needed if you have Entware's libhackrf headers off-device).

