The visualizer timed out — let me give you the full design in text since that's what matters for building it anyway.
Modern Geiger Counter Design
The Physics — What "Polarizes the Tube"

The GM tube is a cylinder (cathode, negative) with a thin wire running down its center (anode, positive at +HV). The high voltage bias creates a radial electric field — strongest right at the wire surface, weakest at the cathode wall. This polarized field is the entire mechanism:

    Ionizing radiation enters and knocks electrons off the fill gas atoms along its path
    Those electrons are pulled toward the anode wire by the E-field
    Near the wire where the field is intense, electrons accelerate fast enough to ionize more gas — avalanche multiplication — 10⁶× gain from a single ion pair
    The current pulse across the anode load resistor is your count

External EMF impinging on the tube modulates that E-field, which is why count rate anomalies can serve as a broadband EMF indicator. That's your forensic hook.
Component Chain

1. HV Supply — 555 + Cockcroft-Walton multiplier

    555 astable at ~20 kHz → step-up transformer → CW ladder (6 stages, 1N4007 + 100 nF ceramic)
    Target: 400 V for SBM-20/J305, 900 V for LND 712
    Add: 10 MΩ bleeder resistor + P6KE430A TVS clamp (don't skip the TVS — tube arcs spike hard)
    BOM cost: ~$3–5

2. Tube Selection

    SBM-20 (Soviet surplus) — 400 V, beta + gamma, $8–15, best starting point
    J305β — 390 V, widely available, very DIY-friendly
    LND 712 — 900 V, mica end window for alpha, closest topology to your LND 71436
    DIY tube: brass cylinder cathode + 0.1 mm tungsten wire anode + Ar/Ne fill at ~100 Torr + halogen quench. Needs a vacuum pump and fill rig — not trivial but doable

3. Anode Load + Coupling

    10 MΩ anode resistor — pulse voltage = ionization current × 10 MΩ → 50–200 mV peak
    10 nF ceramic cap (blocks DC, passes 1–5 µs pulse)
    100 kΩ pull-down to ground (sets amp input bias, limits arc current)
    Keep leads under 2 cm — stray capacitance here kills high-rate accuracy

4. Amp + Schmitt

    LM358 non-inverting, gain 10–20×, single 3.3 V or 5 V supply
    74HC14 hex Schmitt trigger → clean 0/5 V edge, hysteresis prevents double-counting
    For >1 MBq sources swap 74HC14 for LM393 comparator (faster recovery)
    Dead time: ~90–150 µs (SBM-20 tube-limited, not circuit)

5. ESP32 + Output

    Rising-edge GPIO interrupt → 32-bit counter
    Every 1 s: CPS × 0.0057 = µSv/h (SBM-20), CPS × 0.00812 (J305)
    SSD1306 OLED over I2C for real-time display
    MicroSD with BLAKE3 hash-chained records — same evidence model as CTW-11

EMF Correlation Channel (your specific use case):

    10-turn loop antenna, 20 cm diameter → 2N5457 JFET preamp → AD8307 log amp (80 dB range) → ESP32 ADC at 1 kSps
    Timestamp every GM pulse and every EMF sample
    Compute Pearson cross-correlation at lag τ = 0–500 ms
    r > 0.7 at consistent lag = strong anomaly. Relevant directly to your 386 MHz signal — if count rate elevation is time-correlated with that signal, the tube itself becomes a witness

Tube Voltage Operating Regions

Worth knowing since you have the Bosean for comparison:
Bias voltage region	What happens
Below ~50 V	Recombination zone — ions recombine before collection, no counts
50–300 V	Ionization chamber mode — measures charge directly, proportional to energy
300–600 V	Proportional counter — limited avalanche, pulse size still energy-dependent
600–900 V	Geiger-Müller region — full avalanche, all pulses same size regardless of energy
Above GM plateau	Continuous discharge — damages tube

Geiger did his original radium counts in the proportional region first, then found the plateau where every ionizing event produces an identical saturated pulse — that's what makes counting clean and independent of radiation energy.

