ENODEV (errno=19) — the device node exists but the kernel driver behind it is not loaded and not built in. Check the kernel config:
sh

zcat /proc/config.gz | grep TUN

If that comes back empty or CONFIG_TUN=m with no module available, TUN is simply not available on this kernel build. In that case we have two paths:

Option A — check if OpenWrt has a kmod package under a different name:
sh

opkg list | grep -i tun
opkg list | grep -i tap
opkg list | grep kmod | grep -i net

Option B — check what kernel version and look for the module manually:
sh

uname -r
find / -name '*.ko' 2>/dev/null | grep -i tun
find / -name '*.ko.gz' 2>/dev/null | grep -i tun

Option C — if TUN truly isn't available, we pivot the softmac transport layer. Instead of a TUN interface, we can run smac0 as a raw socket or tap into an existing interface via AF_PACKET. The daemon's softmac_platform_stub.c would need a rewrite of softmac_platform_init() to open a raw socket on br-lan instead of /dev/net/tun. This is actually cleaner for a router deployment since the LAN bridge is already there.

