vtun and qtun won't work — those are VPN tunnel apps, not the raw TUN kernel driver. tun2socks is also wrong direction. None of them expose /dev/net/tun to the kernel.

Check the config first to know what we're dealing with:
sh

zcat /proc/config.gz | grep TUN

Then:
sh

opkg list | grep kmod | grep -v Packages | head -40

If CONFIG_TUN is absent entirely, we pivot to Option C — raw socket transport. This is actually the right architecture for a router anyway. Instead of creating a virtual TUN interface, we bind directly to br-lan using AF_PACKET:

The rewrite of softmac_platform_init() would:

    Open a raw AF_PACKET socket on br-lan
    Filter for a custom ethertype (e.g. 0x88B5 — locally assigned)
    All other platform functions stay identical
    No kernel module dependency at all

Want me to write the softmac_platform_stub.c replacement using AF_PACKET now while you check those two commands? It removes the TUN dependency entirely and is actually more appropriate for the router deployment model since smac0 was always going to sit on top of the LAN bridge anyway.

