ASUS RT-AX89X — that's the board. High-end IPQ8074 router. The WiFi DSP crash is a known firmware mismatch issue on this board with OpenWrt. The q6_fw files are present but the chip version check is failing.

First — Dropbear. Interface='lan' is correct, lan UCI maps to br-lan. The IPv6 issue is because IPv6='0' isn't a valid UCI option for dropbear. Do it differently:
sh

# Remove the bad IPv6 option, use GatewayPorts and bind to IPv4 only
uci delete dropbear.@dropbear[0].IPv6 2>/dev/null
uci set dropbear.@dropbear[0].Interface='lan'
uci set dropbear.@dropbear[0].ListenAddress='192.168.1.1'
uci commit dropbear
/etc/init.d/dropbear restart
netstat -tlnp | grep :22

Now the WiFi crash — this is the real fix for the RT-AX89X:
sh

# The crash message says Hardware:0x1 Software:0x2
# The q6 firmware is for hw2.0 but chip is hw1.0
# Check what version the firmware expects
strings /lib/firmware/IPQ8074/q6_fw.mdt | grep -i "version\|chip\|hw\|8074" | head -20

# Check OpenWrt bug tracker version — what version of OpenWrt is this
cat /etc/openwrt_release

# The fix for RT-AX89X is usually a specific firmware blob
# Check if there's a wcss firmware in a different location
find / -name "*.mdt" 2>/dev/null
find / -name "*wcss*" 2>/dev/null

The RT-AX89X has a known issue where the WiFi DSP firmware shipped with some OpenWrt builds targets IPQ8074 hw2.0 but the actual chip in the AX89X is hw1.0. The crash loop has likely been running since boot — crash #86660 means it's been cycling roughly every 400ms since the system started. That's about 10 hours of continuous crash loops.

